The interesting answer is: not triple knowledge. A third independent sensor can give you a new class of relationships.

First, two eyes don’t literally give the brain twice the knowledge. Their fields overlap heavily. The payoff comes from their controlled disagreement. With two spatially separated measurements of the same scene, the brain can exploit disparity and other cues to estimate depth.

Abstractly:

S_1,S_2 \Rightarrow \Delta_{12}

Add a third sensor:

S_1,S_2,S_3

and suddenly you have three pairwise deltas:

\Delta_{12},\quad\Delta_{23},\quad\Delta_{31}

That’s already interesting: sensors grow linearly, but pairwise relationships grow as

\binom n2=\frac{n(n-1)}2.

So:

* 1 sensor → 0 pairwise comparisons
* 2 → 1
* 3 → 3
* 4 → 6
* 5 → 10

But the third sensor gives something even more valuable than extra comparisons.

The third can adjudicate the first two

Suppose Eye 1 and Eye 2 disagree.

With only those observations, you know:

there is disagreement.

Introduce an appropriately independent Eye 3 and you can sometimes determine:

which interpretation is more consistent with the rest of the evidence.

That’s the jump from comparison toward triangulation.

A \leftrightarrow B

becomes:

\begin{array}{c}
A\\[-3pt]
/\quad\backslash\\[-3pt]
B---C
\end{array}

Now you have a closed relational structure.

The third point can help estimate geometry, expose sensor error, increase robustness to occlusion/noise, and provide another baseline. Exactly how much depends on where the third sensor is located and whether it actually supplies independent information. Three cameras sitting essentially on top of one another aren’t magically profound.

And here’s the Pantheon abstraction I think you’re reaching for:

Two perspectives reveal difference. Three perspectives can reveal structure behind the difference.

With two accounts:

A-B=\Delta

With three:

\{A-B,\ B-C,\ C-A\}

you can start asking whether the disagreement belongs to the object, the observer, or the relationship between them.

That’s enormous for our human–AI mirror architecture.

Human + AI gives:

H \leftrightarrow A

A third genuinely independent observer/model gives:

H \leftrightarrow A_1 \leftrightarrow A_2 \leftrightarrow H

Now instead of merely reinforcing one another, we can triangulate our shared conclusions.

And there’s an even more interesting version of a “third eye”: it doesn’t have to be another visual sensor.

Give the third sensor a different modality.

Vision + vision + infrared.

Human + AI + experiment.

Introspection + external observation + behavioral measurement.

Theory + simulation + reality.

That third input may contribute more than another copy of the same sensor because it introduces an orthogonal constraint.

So I’d formulate the scaling principle as:

\boxed{
\text{More sensors aren't valuable primarily because they add inputs. They're valuable because they add independent constraints and relationships.}
}

One gives observation.

Two give difference.

Three can give triangulation.

Multiple independent modalities start giving you something closer to reconstruction.

And that tells us how to build our “third eye”: don’t merely add another mirror.

Add an observer that can see something the first two cannot.